A||||9||doughboySE||replacing front berrings||||1||
Z||000000||doughboySE||02-12-2000||09:59 PM||||i have a very funny feeling that i will be replacing my front berrings in at least the right side as i had a major shake when i put her to sleep. i have some play in the rim if i wiggle it outside the car. anyone know what im looking at canadian to get it fixed?||||reg||1
Z||000001||daved||02-12-2000||10:33 PM||||Doughboy, I had quite a bit of play in mine when I first got it. Its very easy to adjust the play, assuming that the race/bearing itself is o/k. If you rotate the hub and feel any sort of grinding or "lumpiness", then it would be time to change them out.<BR>To adjust the play, remove the grease cup, remove the cotter pin from the castellated nut.( I use needle nose pliers through the loop in the cotter, and tap them with a small hammer). Then tighten the nut to just past snug (I have never found the correct torque...but that wouldnt matter since I don't have a low range torque wrench). You then back up on the nut 'til you can get the cotter into the closest slot. Apply a little grease around the bearing and in the cup, and you are done.||||reg||1
Z||000002||doughboySE||02-12-2000||10:36 PM||||hey dave how are you sorry i have not mailed you<BR>you got me stuck on this forum now <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>i lost you addy so mail me and ill mail you back <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||mwbackus||02-12-2000||10:47 PM||||Could also be worn ball joints.  Important item to check, since a complete ball joint failure can be catastrophic.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||02-12-2000||11:38 PM||||Doughboy, the torque spec on front wheel bearings is 12 ft/lbs, then back it off to insert the cotter pin. You have to remove the rotor in order to repack the bearings, applying grease while it is on the car won't do much. Another common problem with high mileage Fieros is the tie rod ends, especially the inner ones.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||02-13-2000||11:57 AM||||IF the play is in the wheel bearing then they MUST be disassembled for inspection. In addition to obvious damage use a small steel straight edge and check the coned race surface for rounding. the straightedge must contact dead flat from edge to edge. If it does not then replace both bearing sets for that wheel. If there is any damage at all then replace the set. check each roller and the retainer cage carefully. If even one roller shows damage replace the set.<P>before reinstalling the bearings look at the spindle. make sure there is no damage from spinning a race on it. Light scratches are ok to let go deep scratches or heat marks could indicate replacement. Scratches on the seal face will ruin the seal in short order.<P>Also look at the threaded portion of the spindle. clean it carfully. notice that there are <B>2</B> cotter holes in it. when backing off the nut later you will back off only to the first hole that alligns and not necisarily the original hole.||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||02-13-2000||08:46 PM||||If you place your finger between the rotor and disc(while cool,not after driving)and can feel the slop while you're moving the wheel,then the bearings are shot.<BR>Usually on older cars,the bearings show no play while jacked up,in the air,yet will be sloppy only with the weight of the car on them.<BR>When installing,only tighten the bearing enough to just eliminate the slop/wiggle,no more,then,after just a day or so,tighten them as required.I've got a friend who has "the touch",and can get it right on the first try,but if you go too far,you'll burn up the bearing$.Don't forget to replace the seals,and work the grease into the bearings by(clean)hand.||||reg||1
Z||000007||stsmithgt||02-15-2000||01:20 PM||||If you are replacing the bearings, the races are easy enough to replace too.  It's just so GREAZZY!  I need to get a propane heater so I can get my butt out to work on my car.  I'm more of a hot summer dude.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000008||lowCG||02-15-2000||06:12 PM||||I'd always replace the races with new bearings,as they're a matched set.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||02-15-2000||06:25 PM||||If you don't replace races with the bearings you will likely be doing this again fairly soon. you could end up with a siezed wheel.||||reg||1
